    "summary": "Temporal Calibration measures whether a generated video preserves a stable physical clock . When a file declares a playback frame rate, does the motion visible in the frames imply the same real-time progression? WorldArena applies this as an output-only audit in the real time dimension using the wrapped Pulse-of-Motion / Visual Chronometer predictor [Gao et al., 2026]. This is not the same as optical flow, motion magnitude, or temporal smoothness. A rollout can look smooth frame to frame and still be temporally wrong: falls may drift as if gravity were slowed, or fast periodic motion may appear unnaturally sluggish. Visual Chronometer calls this failure mode chronometric hallucination —visually coherent dynamics with an ambiguous, unstable, or uncontrollable physical time scale. The predictor estimates Physical FPS (PhyFPS) from overlapping temporal windows instead of trusting container metadata alone. WorldArena compares the mean inferred rate with the nominal playback FPS $f {\\mathrm{meta}}$ (OpenCV container FPS, or manifest fallback) and reports three official metrics: physical fps error, physical fps pct error, and physical fps intra video cv. The first two capture global tempo mismatch; the third captures rhythm drift inside a single clip. For each prediction, the runtime extracts overlapping windows with defaults clip length = 30, stride = 4, and resolution = 216. Each window yields a segment estimate $\\hat{f} k$. The mean inferred physical FPS is \\[ \\bar{f} {\\mathrm{phy}} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum {k=1}^{K} \\hat{f} k \\tag{1} \\] Absolute and relative calibration errors are \\[ E {\\mathrm{abs}} = \\left|\\bar{f} {\\mathrm{phy}} - f {\\mathrm{meta}}\\right| \\tag{2} \\] \\[ E {\\mathrm{pct}} = \\frac{E {\\mathrm{abs}}}{f {\\mathrm{meta}}} \\tag{3} \\] Within-video instability is the coefficient of variation over segment predictions: \\[ C {\\mathrm{intra}} = \\frac{\\operatorname{std}(\\hat{f} 1,\\ldots,\\hat{f} K)}{\\operatorname{mean}(\\hat{f} 1,\\ldots,\\hat{f} K)} \\tag{4} \\] WorldArena maps physical fps error, physical fps pct error, and physical fps intra video cv to $[0,1]$ with fixed reverse scaling (physical fps error: $[0,30]$; the other two: $[0,1]$). Higher normalized values mean better temporal calibration. When the side runtime, checkpoint, or FPS metadata is unavailable, the metric family is reported as not applicable.",
